  "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x-5) & =2 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-5)} & ={3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-5)}=x-5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x-5 & =9 \\\\ x & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
